// src/v4/extdeps/languages/llvm_ir.dag
//
// Scope: LLVM IR model — direction-agnostic. Typed SSA intermediate
//        representation: modules, functions, basic blocks, SSA values,
//        instructions, types. A LOWER-level language than rust/py/ts.
// Anchor: LLVM Language Reference Manual — pin a concrete release
//         (e.g. LLVM 18 LangRef) as the versioned fidelity authority
//         (L-2: model the spec, not a library; the spec IS the anchor).
// Stress (T-4.12 — a B2-OMNI falsification probe): does the Node
//   substrate + B2-OMNI generalize DOWN the abstraction stack, not just
//   across surface languages? SSA form (every value defined once;
//   phi nodes; dominance) is a structural shape unlike source ASTs. If
//   LLVM IR cannot be a plain `LanguageModel` without core/pipeline
//   changes, B2-OMNI is leaking — surfaced now, before load-bearing.
// Clear win: `.dag → LLVM IR` (then LLVM lowers to machine code), AND
//   `LLVM IR → Node` ingest — the down-stack half of O(N+M).
// C5-fidelity note: F here is essentially all-structural — LLVM IR has
//   negligible cosmetic surface, so `emit∘ingest` is ~identity (no
//   trivia to normalize). Contrast source models where F may include
//   comments/formatting by intent.
//
// Owns:
//   - LLVM IR grammar as declarative bidirectional data (B2-OMNI):
//     module / function / basic-block / instruction / SSA value / type
//   - SSA + dominance modeled structurally (NOT a 6th behavior — the
//     value graph is Node edges; phi is a Node, per IN-B discipline)
//   - Inhabitance: LLVM integer/float/vector/pointer types per std/algebra.dag
//   - Cost realization: per-instruction cost on an LLVM target
//
// Consumes:
//   - std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag, std/primitive.dag
//
// Status: T-4.12 modeled 2026-05-16 (snappy-badger-570). B2-OMNI probe
//   RESULT: PASS — LLVM IR is a plain declarative LanguageModel over the
//   closed Node substrate with NO 6th behavior and NO core/pipeline
//   change (see modeling notes "B2-OMNI probe finding"). The header I/O
//   contract is immutable for downstream workers; the `std/primitive.dag`
//   line in Consumes is a pre-T-1-closeout staleness (primitive.dag was
//   decomposed; `Symbol`/`Hash` re-homed to std/node.dag per PR #3152 —
//   this file imports from node.dag accordingly; surfaced, not edited,
//   since the contract lines are immutable and the re-home is already
//   operator-ratified). Splitting this file requires explicit operator
//   ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.languages.llvm_ir


// std/node.dag is imported: it makes the K-1 opaque `Symbol` (the
// name-reference identity used pervasively in the grammar — block
// labels, SSA/parameter/global names) resolvable through this file's
// import closure. std/algebra.dag is NOT imported: this file's declared
// `Consumes` of it is its INHABITANCE, which is DEFERRED to the
// operator-pending D2 STOP (see the Inhabitance section). The algebra
// import + the ratified instance-value form are added only once D2
// lands; importing algebra now while unused would be dead code, and
// improvising the instances now would be the exact per-file
// `<Algebra><Concrete>` form D2 forbids.
import v4.std.node { Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// SPEC, NOT LIBRARY (M3 / L-2). Anchor = the LLVM Language Reference
// Manual, pinned at LLVM 18. The model is the LangRef grammar + type
// system + value/instruction taxonomy. LLVM intrinsics, target
// libraries, and pass-specific metadata are NOT modeled — an intrinsic
// call is just a `Call` whose callee is a named function (a program in
// the modeled language = a `Node`), exactly as M3 / the T-4 authoring
// contract requires. Modeling the intrinsic/library universe is infinite
// and the wrong layer.
//
// DECLARATIVE MODEL, NOT A RECOGNIZER (T-4 authoring contract). This
// file is the grammar/type/semantics as DATA. It declares no
// `ingest`/`emit` procedure: those are the GENERIC walker (compiler
// 01_tokenize/02_parse/05_emit, B2-OMNI) reading this model. Concretely
// this is why the file consumes neither a `Result<T, Diagnostic>` carrier
// nor std/diagnostic.dag: there is an open operator-tier STOP on the
// absence of a shared value-or-Diagnostic carrier in src/v4 (first
// surfaced by T-4.6); a declarative LanguageModel does not need it and
// must not improvise one. Fail-closed behavior off the lossless core is
// the walker's obligation; this file's contribution is to DECLARE the
// lossless core F structurally (see `FidelityDisposition`), which is
// pure node.dag Disj data — no Diagnostic, no Result.
//
// B2-OMNI PROBE FINDING (the deliverable — the STOP discipline is the
// product). LLVM IR models as a plain `LanguageModel` with NO 6th
// behavior and NO core/pipeline change. The structurally-unusual SSA
// shapes resolve to ORDINARY Node structure — and modeling them HONESTLY
// drove the right structural form:
//   - "every value defined once" (SSA) — the value graph (operands
//     reference their producers) realized the A1-FAITHFUL, FINITE way:
//     a value-producing instruction / φ carries `result: Symbol` (its
//     def-site name); an operand is a NAME-REFERENCE
//     (`LlvmValue.LocalRef`) resolved through function scope to that
//     def-site (or a parameter / a global / an inline constant). This
//     IS the scaffold's "value graph is Node edges" — node.dag A1's
//     cross-declaration idiom (references are name `Atom`s resolved
//     through the namespace; precedent: diagnostic.dag `reason:
//     Symbol`). Single-assignment is the structural fact that exactly
//     one def-site declares each name (INVARIANTS P2). CRUCIAL: the
//     def-use graph is CYCLIC for every loop (a loop-carried φ names an
//     instruction whose operand names the φ) — a name-reference keeps
//     the bounded Node tree FINITE while expressing that cycle, which is
//     PRECISELY why A1 mandates name-reference recursion. An EARLIER cut
//     INLINED the producer node into each use (`InstructionValue {
//     instruction }` / `PhiValue { phi }`); that could not finitely
//     represent cyclic loop-carried SSA — it excluded ALL loops, a
//     P1/M3 spec-narrowing and the exact A1 violation A1 exists to
//     prevent. The name-reference form is the correct finite
//     REALIZATION of the value-graph intent (operands → producers), not
//     an abandonment of it. `result: Symbol` is a
//     declaration FIELD = a name-reference (K-1-opaque, resolved
//     structurally), NOT a runtime Symbol mint — the K-1 no-mint rule
//     was specific to the removed inhabitance LAMBDAS, not declaration-
//     class fields. The textual `%x`/`%1` spelling is `DeclaredNormalized`
//     (α-equivalent — `Symbol` is opaque, the content-hash fold is
//     rename-invariant).
//   - phi — phi is an ORDINARY Node, hoisted to the block's structural
//     `phis` region (`PhiNode`), so "a phi after a non-phi instruction"
//     is UNREPRESENTABLE (P2), not a checker rule. It is NOT a variant
//     of the general instruction sum (its block-start position is
//     structural). At the computation layer phi is `Branch`-shaped (it
//     selects an incoming value by which CFG edge was taken) — it reuses
//     the EXISTING closed `Behavior::Branch`, it does not add one. Per
//     IN-B: control/data flow is Node edges; phi is a Node.
//   - dominance — NOT a field or flag (feedback_no_annotations). The CFG
//     is carried structurally (each block's terminator names its
//     successor labels — `block_successors`). "Definition dominates all
//     uses" is a LENS read over that Node graph (the same shape as the
//     complexity/effect lenses), derived, never stored; a `dominates`
//     edge would be a second authority for what the CFG already
//     determines (INVARIANTS P2).
//
// A2 TOTALITY IS A `.dag`-PROGRAM PROPERTY, NOT A MODELED-LANGUAGE ONE.
// An LLVM CFG can contain irreducible / non-terminating loops (a back-
// edge `br` to a dominating block). That is faithful to LangRef and is
// MODELED here as ordinary CFG data — std/node.dag A2 (Loop is bounded,
// total-by-construction) constrains `.dag` programs the compiler
// ACCEPTS, never the external languages it models/emits (M3: extdeps
// model real specs). `emit (.dag → LLVM IR)` only ever emits the bounded
// forms a `.dag` program can express; ingesting arbitrary LLVM whose
// termination is not structurally evident is the walker's fail-closed
// boundary (declared in F below), not a totality this file may claim.
//
// INHABITANCE IS OWNED ELSEWHERE (P2) — see the "Inhabitance — OWNED
// ELSEWHERE" section near the end. This file declares only the LLVM
// type/value/instruction VOCABULARY; WHICH algebra a scalar grounds in
// is owned by the std/ scalar wave (std/integer.dag/float.dag/logic.dag,
// T-3) + the operator-pending D2 FORM decision, by P2 single-authority.
// This file does NOT declare, assert, or restate that grounding (an
// extdeps language file restating it is a second-authority P2
// violation).
// Faithful observations for that owner / the D2 decision (NOT this
// file's authority): LLVM `iN` arithmetic wraps mod 2^N ⇒ a finite ring
// that is NOT order-compatible (the algebra.dag Float≠Field analog);
// `fcmp` is NaN-unordered; pointers have no total numeric algebra.
//
// Every coproduct below carries the full Practice-4 🟢/🟡/🔴
// classification + the 5-pattern dissolution ledger (modeling-
// discipline.md §4 — operator-ratified binding 2026-05-15; FIVE patterns
// attempted, the fifth being Parameterized-family / Practice-7).
// Records (Conj) carry no ledger. The closed opcode/predicate enums are
// the LangRef-defined sets: an addition is an upstream LangRef revision
// (the M3 spec-anchor analog of std/node.dag's C1 stop-signal — a
// visible, ratified edit, never a silent `Other`/`...`).


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FloatKind — the LLVM floating-point type set (LangRef "Floating-Point
// Types"). Closed: half / bfloat / float / double / fp128 / x86_fp80 /
// ppc_fp128.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every consumer that handles a float type
//      (inhabitance, emit, cost) dispatches on the kind uniformly; it is
//      not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: promoting to `{ exponent_bits,
//      mantissa_bits }` looks uniform but x86_fp80 / ppc_fp128 are NOT
//      mechanical (80-bit extended has an explicit integer bit;
//      ppc_fp128 is a double-double, not an IEEE format) — a numeric
//      record makes "an IEEE format with a double-double layout"
//      representable, the inverse of P2. The named LangRef set is the
//      faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: this IS the modeled LangRef set (M3); there
//      is no richer std/ source it traces to — it is the source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a float type is EXACTLY ONE kind; never
//      several at once — alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the seven are not `F<X>` over a
//      separately-declared set (x86_fp80 / ppc_fp128 break any uniform
//      width projection — see pattern 2); not a Practice-7 projection.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef floating-point type set.
type FloatKind
  = HalfFloat
  | BFloatFloat
  | SingleFloat
  | DoubleFloat
  | Fp128Float
  | X86Fp80Float
  | PpcFp128Float


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LlvmType — the LLVM type system (LangRef "Type System"). Recursive:
// aggregate / pointer / vector / function types contain types. This is a
// downstream domain type (surface sugar over node.dag Conj/Disj), NOT a
// second recursive kernel type — exactly the std/algebra.dag
// `FreeMonoid<T>` precedent (a recursive generic sum is admissible
// downstream; node.dag A1 closes the KERNEL, not domain models).
//
//   - VoidType / LabelType / TokenType / MetadataType — the non-value
//     and special types (LangRef).
//   - IntegerType { bits } — `iN`, any bit width ≥ 1 (LangRef: arbitrary
//     precision). `bits` is a kernel-ambient `Int`; the i1..i128 spellings
//     are NOT enumerated siblings — they are this one type projected over
//     the width (Practice 7 / INVARIANTS P1 integer worked example).
//   - FloatingType { kind } — projects over the closed `FloatKind`.
//   - PointerType { address_space } — LLVM ≥ 15 OPAQUE pointer: it
//     carries no pointee (that was removed from LangRef); address space
//     is a kernel-ambient `Int` (0 = default).
//   - VectorType { element, count, scalable } — `<N x T>` /
//     `<vscale x N x T>`. `scalable` is a genuine 2-valued LangRef
//     distinction (fixed vs scalable), modeled as the closed
//     `VectorScaling` (not a bare Bool — keeps "scalable fixed-vector"
//     unrepresentable, P2).
//   - ArrayType { element, count } — `[N x T]`.
//   - StructType { fields, packed } — literal struct; `packed`
//     (`<{ }>`) is the closed `StructPacking`.
//   - FunctionType { result, params, var_arg } — `RetTy (Args...)`;
//     `var_arg` is the closed `VarArg` (C-style `...`).
//   - NamedType { name } — a reference to a module-level identified
//     struct, by opaque `Symbol` (K-1) resolved through the module
//     namespace; this is how LLVM expresses recursive/forward struct
//     types (`%T = type { ptr, %T* }`) WITHOUT a self-referential type
//     definition here — the recursion is the namespace edge, exactly
//     node.dag A1's name-reference recursion discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every type consumer (inhabitance, emit,
//      cost, GEP index walking) branches on the type category; it is the
//      shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the categories carry disjoint shapes
//      (IntegerType { bits }; VectorType { element, count, scalable };
//      VoidType nullary). A flat `{ tag, bits?, element?, … }` record
//      makes "VoidType with bits" and "IntegerType with an element type"
//      representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: this IS the modeled LangRef type taxonomy
//      (M3 spec anchor); there is no richer std/ algebra it traces to —
//      it is the grounding source other LLVM structure stands on.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an LLVM type is EXACTLY ONE category;
//      genuine alternatives, never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the categories are structurally
//      heterogeneous (scalar widths vs aggregates vs function vs opaque
//      pointer vs named reference) — NOT one `F<X>` over a declared set.
//      The width AXIS *inside* IntegerType IS a projection (pattern-5
//      applied there: `bits: Int`, not enumerated iN siblings), but the
//      category sum itself is not a mechanical copy of any set.
// Terminal: the MODELED LangRef (LLVM 18) type taxonomy — void/label/
// token/metadata + the integer/float/pointer scalar core + the
// aggregate/vector/function/named constructors. A LangRef type OUTSIDE
// this modeled set (e.g. `x86_amx`, a `target("…")` target-extension
// type) is the EXPLICIT `FidelityDisposition.FailClosed` boundary, the
// concrete machine-consumable `FidelityFeature.UnmodeledType`
// (`feature_disposition` maps it to `FailClosed`) — declared,
// diagnosed, never a silent fabricated parse (no-engine):
// completeness of F is declared, not over-claimed. Extending the modeled
// core is an upstream-LangRef-revision edit to THIS declaration (the M3
// spec-anchor analog of node.dag's C1 stop-signal), never a silent open
// variant.
//
// RAW-Int WIDTH RESIDUAL — TRACKED 🟡 SCAFFOLD (isolated, the exact
// std/diagnostic.dag `Locus`(🟢)/`Extent.ByteRange`(🟡) precedent). The
// SUM is 🟢 terminal (the categories are genuine), but the numeric
// payloads `IntegerType.bits` / `VectorType.count` / `ArrayType.count` /
// `PointerType.address_space` are kernel-ambient `Int`, which
// STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal LLVM values (a 0-bit / negative-width
// integer, a negative element count) — modeling-discipline.md §2
// (illegal states unrepresentable) forbids it, and no bounded-natural
// substrate is consumable yet (std/cardinality.dag refinement +
// std/nat.dag are parallel Wave-A1, NOT in this file's whitelist). It is
// a TRACKED scaffold declared with the three bridge properties, verbatim
// the merged diagnostic.dag discipline (PR #3161):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the raw `Int` widths are provisional,
//       not the terminal bounded-width shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only LangRef-valid
//       widths (`bits >= 1`; `count >= 0`; `address_space >= 0`). Until
//       the substrate enforces it the invariant is producer-side; an
//       out-of-range width is a producer bug, not a legal value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (its own T-3 entry), these refine to bounded
//       non-negative widths type-enforced, and this residual
//       reclassifies 🟢 (the SUM is already 🟢; only the payload moves).
type LlvmType
  = VoidType
  | LabelType
  | TokenType
  | MetadataType
  | IntegerType { bits: Int }
  | FloatingType { kind: FloatKind }
  | PointerType { address_space: Int }
  | VectorType { element: LlvmType, count: Int, scalable: VectorScaling }
  | ArrayType { element: LlvmType, count: Int }
  | StructType { fields: List<LlvmType>, packed: StructPacking }
  | FunctionType { result: LlvmType, params: List<LlvmType>, var_arg: VarArg }
  | NamedType { name: Symbol }


// VectorScaling — `<N x T>` (fixed) vs `<vscale x N x T>` (scalable,
// SVE/RVV-style). A closed LangRef distinction, modeled as a sum rather
// than a Bool so that no third state is representable and the spelling
// difference is structural, not a flag (feedback_no_annotations).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a vector's scaling is read uniformly
//      wherever a vector type is consumed (emit, cost); not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the only "data" reduction is a Bool,
//      which is the M4 stringly/boolean-proxy anti-pattern for a closed
//      named LangRef distinction; the two-name sum is the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: it is a leaf LangRef spelling distinction;
//      no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a vector type is fixed XOR scalable, never
//      both — alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous nullary spellings,
//      not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed fixed-vs-scalable LangRef distinction.
type VectorScaling
  = FixedVector
  | ScalableVector


// StructPacking — `{ }` (natural alignment) vs `<{ }>` (packed, byte-
// aligned). Closed LangRef distinction; same rationale as VectorScaling.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: packing is read uniformly by layout/emit/
//      cost consumers; not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reduces only to a Bool — the M4 boolean-
//      proxy for a closed named spelling; the two-name sum is faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef spelling distinction; no
//      richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a struct is packed XOR unpacked; never both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two nullary spellings, not `F<X>`.
// Terminal: the closed packed-vs-unpacked LangRef distinction.
type StructPacking
  = NaturalAligned
  | Packed


// VarArg — a function type either is variadic (`...`) or is not. Closed
// LangRef distinction; same Bool-proxy rationale as above.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: variadicity is read uniformly by call/
//      emit/ABI consumers; not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reduces only to a Bool — M4 boolean-
//      proxy; the two-name sum is faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef spelling distinction.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a function type is variadic XOR fixed-arity.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two nullary spellings, not `F<X>`.
// Terminal: the closed variadic-vs-fixed LangRef distinction.
type VarArg
  = FixedArity
  | Variadic


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LlvmConstant — compile-time constants (LangRef "Constants" /
// "Complex Constants" / "Constant Expressions"). Recursive: aggregate
// constants nest constants; a constant expression is a frozen, side-
// effect-free instruction-shaped form.
//
//   - IntConstant { ty, value } / FloatConstant { ty, bits } —
//     `value`/`bits` are kernel-ambient `Int` (the bit-pattern; the
//     float literal value is the IEEE bit pattern, not a lossy decimal —
//     the C5 lossless-core discipline).
//   - NullPointer { ty } / NoneToken — `null` / `none`.
//   - AggregateConstant { ty, elements } — struct/array/vector literal.
//   - ZeroInitializer { ty } — `zeroinitializer` (whole-aggregate zero;
//     a distinct LangRef spelling, not an all-zero AggregateConstant —
//     keeping them distinct is faithful to the spec surface).
//   - UndefValue { ty } / PoisonValue { ty } — `undef` / `poison` (two
//     genuinely distinct LangRef deferred-UB forms; NOT one flagged
//     case — collapsing makes the UB semantics indistinguishable).
//   - BlockAddress { function, block } — `blockaddress(@f, %bb)`,
//     name-references (K-1 Symbols) into the namespace.
//   - GlobalAddress { name } — the address of a module global, a
//     name-reference Symbol.
//   - ConstantExpr { op, operands, result_type } — the restricted
//     constant-expression grammar (LangRef): a Practice-7 projection
//     over the constexpr-admissible opcode subset (`ConstExprOpcode`),
//     NOT a re-enumeration of the instruction set.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: a constant is consumed uniformly wherever
//      an operand/initializer is read; not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: disjoint shapes (IntConstant { ty,
//      value }; AggregateConstant { ty, elements }; NoneToken nullary).
//      A flat record makes "a NoneToken with elements" representable —
//      the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — the NUMERIC constants' algebra grounding is
//      OWNED ELSEWHERE (std/ scalar wave + pending D2, P2 — see the
//      Inhabitance section; not declared here) — and the SUM itself is
//      the LangRef constant taxonomy, not one algebra carrier; N/A as a
//      whole-sum dissolution.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a constant is EXACTLY ONE form; never
//      several simultaneously.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the forms are heterogeneous (scalar
//      / aggregate / deferred-UB / address / constexpr); NOT one `F<X>`
//      over a declared set. The constexpr OPCODE axis IS a projection
//      (pattern-5 applied inside ConstantExpr), but the form sum is not.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef constant taxonomy.
type LlvmConstant
  = IntConstant { ty: LlvmType, value: Int }
  | FloatConstant { ty: LlvmType, bits: Int }
  | NullPointer { ty: LlvmType }
  | NoneToken
  | AggregateConstant { ty: LlvmType, elements: List<LlvmConstant> }
  | ZeroInitializer { ty: LlvmType }
  | UndefValue { ty: LlvmType }
  | PoisonValue { ty: LlvmType }
  | BlockAddress { function: Symbol, block: Symbol }
  | GlobalAddress { name: Symbol }
  | ConstantExpr { op: ConstExprOpcode, operands: List<LlvmConstant>, result_type: LlvmType }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LlvmValue — an SSA operand. THE SSA crux, modeled as a NAME-REFERENCE
// (node.dag A1's cross-declaration idiom — precedent: diagnostic.dag
// `reason: Symbol`): an operand naming its def-site by the K-1-opaque
// `Symbol`, resolved through function scope. This is the FINITE,
// A1-faithful REALIZATION of the value-graph intent (operands reference
// their producers): because the def-use graph is CYCLIC for every loop
// (a loop-carried phi references an instruction that references the
// phi), the reference MUST be a name (finite Node tree, cycle expressed
// through scope) — A1 mandates name-reference recursion for exactly this
// reason. An earlier cut INLINED the producer node; that could not
// finitely represent cyclic loop-carried SSA (excluding ALL loops — a
// P1/M3 spec-narrowing, the A1 violation A1 exists to prevent). The
// name-reference here PRESERVES the value-graph (operands → producers);
// it is its correct realization, not an abandonment.
//   - LocalRef { name } — a use of an in-function def-site by its
//     `Symbol` name, resolved through function scope to a value-
//     producing instruction's `result`, a `PhiNode.result`, or a
//     function parameter (all share LLVM's local `%`-namespace).
//     Single-assignment is the structural fact that exactly one def-site
//     declares each name (INVARIANTS P2); a use is finite (a name), so a
//     def-use CYCLE is representable (the loop-carried-phi case).
//   - ConstantOperand { constant } — an inline `LlvmConstant`.
//   - GlobalRef { name } — a module-level global/function address by
//     `Symbol` name, resolved through the module namespace.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every instruction operand is resolved
//      uniformly; the value kind is a shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the variants carry disjoint payloads (a
//      local-scope `Symbol`; an `LlvmConstant`; a module-scope
//      `Symbol`); a flat `{ kind, name?, constant? }` makes "a LocalRef
//      carrying a constant" representable — the inverse of P2. (`LocalRef`
//      and `GlobalRef` are NOT one labelled case: the resolution SCOPE
//      differs — function vs module namespace — a real distinction, not
//      a spelling.)
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: operand-kind is the LangRef value-use
//      taxonomy; no richer std/ source — it is the boundary itself.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an operand is EXACTLY ONE kind; alternatives,
//      not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: heterogeneous payloads / scopes (a
//      function-scope name / a constant / a module-scope name); not one
//      `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the irreducible SSA operand kinds; single-assignment is the
// one-def-site-per-name structural fact (INVARIANTS P2), and the
// name-reference keeps the Node tree finite while admitting cyclic
// def-use (loops) — the A1-faithful value-graph realization.
type LlvmValue
  = LocalRef { name: Symbol }
  | ConstantOperand { constant: LlvmConstant }
  | GlobalRef { name: Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Opcode projection sets (Practice 7 — "the bidirectional relation read
// as a projection over a closed set", not N hand-enumerated instruction
// variants). Each is a closed LangRef set; an addition is an upstream
// LangRef revision (M3 spec-anchor stop-signal), never a silent `Other`.

// BinaryOpcode — integer/float/bitwise binary ops (LangRef "Binary
// Operations" + "Bitwise Binary Operations"). One instruction SHAPE
// (`Binary` below) is projected over this set — adding a binary op costs
// 1 (extend this set), not a new instruction variant (cost-of-change 1,
// CLAUDE.md / Practice 7).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the opcode is read by every binary-
//      instruction consumer (emit, cost, inhabitance dispatch); shared,
//      not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — N/A in the dissolving direction: this set IS
//      the "label promoted to data" result (Practice-2 already applied)
//      — it is the projected axis of the `Binary` shape, the faithful
//      form, not a coproduct awaiting promotion.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL and DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN as the
//      carrier: add/sub/mul DO trace to the abstract algebra, but that
//      grounding is OWNED ELSEWHERE (std/ scalar wave + pending D2, P2 —
//      see the Inhabitance section; not declared here). The *instruction
//      opcode* is the concrete-syntax token the bidirectional grammar
//      needs — the LangRef surface, faithfully named; its algebra
//      grounding is the std/ owner's authority, NOT restated here (two
//      faithful facts in two owners, never a duplicate authority).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a binary instruction has EXACTLY ONE opcode.
//   5. Parameterized family — APPLIED: this enum IS the closed source
//      set the single `Binary` shape projects over (Practice 7); it is
//      not itself further dissolvable — it is the dissolution target.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef binary-opcode set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this projection set was correctly GREEN (the std/ algebra
// inhabitance was "Owned ELSEWHERE" / not-yet-landed in pattern 3).
// Under the post-#3200 consumer-INDEPENDENT rule this is 🟡 YELLOW
// because a namable richer source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: each LLVM binary opcode names ONE algebra operation
//     on the operand-type's inhabitance. The richer source SPLITS into
//     two distinct facts per M9/INVARIANTS P1 — (a) the landed std/
//     algebra.dag STRUCTURE ONTOLOGY the opcode would inhabit, and
//     (b) the per-(type, op) WITNESS, which may or may not yet be
//     landed at the inhabitant. Conflating the two is the trap
//     (briansrls + codex BLOCKING on prior 83afba81/69392ed80 caught
//     this).
//     Landed-witness inventory on origin/main (verified, FIVE in
//     total across std/algebra.dag-inhabiting carriers): (1)
//     int_abelian_group: AbelianGroup<Int> (std/integer.dag:234),
//     (2) int_ordered_ring: OrderedRing<Int> (std/integer.dag:244 —
//     int_abelian_group is its `add:` sub-component per integer.dag:
//     246; one Int-arithmetic story, not two), (3)
//     bool_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Bool> (std/logic.dag:120),
//     (4) nat_additive_commutative_monoid: CommutativeMonoid<Nat>
//     (std/nat.dag:151), (5) nat_semiring: CommutativeSemiring<Nat>
//     (std/nat.dag:174). The Nat witnesses are NOT a richer source
//     for any LLVM iN opcode (LLVM iN ≅ Z/2^N-modular ≠ ℕ); they
//     would mis-route any meaning consumer.
//     (a) LANDED ONTOLOGY (the structure types exist on origin/main):
//       - OpAdd / OpMul → Semiring (std/algebra.dag:185); ontology
//         landed; per-iN-width witness deferred to std/integer.dag D2
//         fan-out (the abstract-Int story rides int_ordered_ring's
//         `add:` / `mul:` sub-components, but no per-width iN
//         witness — and the Nat witnesses are not applicable per the
//         inventory note above).
//       - OpSub → Ring (std/algebra.dag:197 — additive inverse, which
//         Semiring lacks); ontology landed; per-iN-width witness
//         deferred to D2 fan-out (rides int_abelian_group on abstract
//         Int; no per-width iN witness, no Nat applicability).
//       - OpAnd / OpOr / OpXor → BooleanAlgebra (std/algebra.dag:291);
//         landed data witness on origin/main = `bool_boolean_algebra:
//         BooleanAlgebra<Bool>` (std/logic.dag:120). The per-iN
//         bitwise inhabitance (iN-as-Bool^N) is deferred to the D2
//         fan-out — no `data: BooleanAlgebra<iN>` witness landed.
//       - OpFAdd / OpFSub / OpFMul / OpFDiv → ApproximateField
//         (std/algebra.dag:257); machine-readable per-Float witness is
//         EXPLICITLY WITHHELD on origin/main per std/float.dag
//         DECISIONS.md D3 / INVARIANTS P1/P3 (std/float.dag:136 —
//         "machine-readable `data …: ApproximateField<Float>` witness
//         is NOT encoded in this file"). The ontology binding holds;
//         the witness lands in a later std/float.dag phase.
//     (b) NAMED-MISSING CARRIERS (no landed std/algebra.dag ontology
//     match — future authority + trigger named here):
//       - OpSDiv / OpUDiv / OpSRem / OpURem → integer
//         Euclidean-division-with-rounding-mode authority. Ring has
//         NO quotient/remainder operation (a Ring with zero-divisors
//         like `Z/2^N` is not a Field; integer division is a separate
//         algebraic structure, EuclideanDomain-like). MISSING future
//         authority = std/integer.dag bounded-integer-division carrier
//         (truncating / signed-vs-unsigned rounding mode discipline);
//         trigger = first consumer of integer-division MEANING.
//       - OpShl / OpLShr / OpAShr → bit-shift authority. NOT a Lattice
//         operation (meet/join are bitwise AND/OR, not shifts). MISSING
//         future authority = std/integer.dag bit-shift-action carrier
//         (Monoid action of Nat on iN with width-bounded modular
//         reduction; OpAShr selects sign-preserving, OpLShr unsigned-
//         fill); trigger = first consumer of shift MEANING.
//       - OpFRem → IEEE-754 float-remainder authority. ApproximateField
//         has NO remainder operation. MISSING future authority = std/
//         float.dag IEEE-754-remainder op (distinct from the
//         ApproximateField surface); trigger = first consumer of
//         float-remainder MEANING.
//   Trigger: first consumer of binary-op MEANING (cost model / algebraic
//     simplification / inhabitance dispatch — D2 pathway).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the structural opcode →
//     (algebra-carrier, algebra-op) mapping AT that consumer (a lens
//     read), NOT a local match-over-opcode lookup in this carrier; the
//     LangRef surface name stays the parse/emit-side authority.
type BinaryOpcode
  = OpAdd | OpSub | OpMul | OpUDiv | OpSDiv | OpURem | OpSRem
  | OpShl | OpLShr | OpAShr | OpAnd | OpOr | OpXor
  | OpFAdd | OpFSub | OpFMul | OpFDiv | OpFRem


// ConversionOpcode — the cast/conversion ops (LangRef "Conversion
// Operations"). One `Conversion` shape projects over this set.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by every conversion
//      consumer; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — N/A dissolving: this IS the promoted-label
//      projected axis of the `Conversion` shape (Practice-2 applied).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a conversion is a representation change at
//      the machine boundary, not an algebra operation on a carrier; no
//      richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a conversion instruction has EXACTLY ONE
//      opcode.
//   5. Parameterized family — APPLIED: the closed source set the single
//      `Conversion` shape projects over (Practice 7); the dissolution
//      target itself.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef conversion-opcode set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this projection set was correctly GREEN. Under the post-#3200
// consumer-INDEPENDENT rule this is 🟡 YELLOW because a namable richer
// source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: a three-axis classifier {source-domain ∈
//     std/integer.dag (signed/unsigned bounded-Int) | std/float.dag |
//     std/ptr.dag, target-domain ∈ same, kind ∈ sign-extend |
//     zero-extend | truncate | reinterpret-no-op | fp-narrow |
//     fp-widen | fp↔int | ptr↔int | address-space-cast}, where the
//     fp↔int axis ADDITIONALLY discriminates {int-signedness ∈ signed |
//     unsigned} (LangRef separates sitofp from uitofp and fptosi from
//     fptoui precisely on this signedness). LangRef invalid-input
//     disposition is NOT carried by these opcodes — fptoui/fptosi/
//     uitofp/sitofp on out-of-range inputs are UB per LangRef
//     "Conversion Operations" (the saturating fp↔int forms are the
//     `llvm.fptoui.sat` / `llvm.fptosi.sat` intrinsics, distinct
//     declarations outside this opcode set; reachable only via
//     `LlvmIntrinsicCall`, NEVER via this enum). The 13 LangRef cast
//     opcodes are the discriminant-projection of (src-domain,
//     dst-domain, kind, int-signedness) onto LangRef's enumerated
//     names.
//   Invalid-input-disposition deferral: out-of-range / NaN
//     disposition on fp↔int is EXPLICITLY deferred to the first D2
//     meaning-consumer (it pairs with the existing FidelityDisposition
//     machinery; the opcode-level decomposition above carries shape,
//     not disposition).
//   Trigger: first consumer of conversion MEANING (cost model /
//     numeric-precision lens / inhabitance dispatch — D2 pathway).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the (src-domain,
//     dst-domain, kind) decomposition AT that consumer (a lens read),
//     NOT a local match-over-opcode lookup; the LangRef surface name
//     stays the parse/emit-side authority.
type ConversionOpcode
  = OpTrunc | OpZExt | OpSExt
  | OpFPTrunc | OpFPExt
  | OpFPToUI | OpFPToSI | OpUIToFP | OpSIToFP
  | OpPtrToInt | OpIntToPtr | OpBitCast | OpAddrSpaceCast


// IntPredicate — `icmp` predicates (LangRef). The order codomain is
// std/algebra.dag's `Ordering` (consumed via inhabitance, not re-spelled).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the predicate is read uniformly by the
//      `icmp` consumer; shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: promoting to `{ signed: Bool, op: ... }`
//      reintroduces the M4 boolean-proxy AND makes `eq`/`ne` (which have
//      no signedness) carry a meaningless `signed` — illegal-state, P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: the RESULT is `Ordering` (trichotomy,
//      grounded in algebra.dag), but the PREDICATE is the LangRef
//      surface token selecting which trichotomy cell(s) yield true;
//      faithfully the spec set, with its order grounding carried by the
//      comparison's Ordering codomain — not a duplicate authority.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an `icmp` has EXACTLY ONE predicate.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the ten are not `F<X>` over a
//      separately-declared set (signed/unsigned/equality are not a
//      single uniform projection — see pattern 2).
// Terminal: the closed LangRef integer-comparison predicate set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this projection set was correctly GREEN (pattern 3 already noted
// the std/algebra.dag `Ordering` grounding as "Owned ELSEWHERE"). Under
// the post-#3200 consumer-INDEPENDENT rule this is 🟡 YELLOW because
// a namable richer source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: {signedness ∈ Signedness, op ∈ Eq | Order-cell-set}
//     over std/algebra.dag's `Ordering` trichotomy. icmp eq/ne project
//     from the `Eq` view; icmp ult/ule/ugt/uge from the unsigned-Order
//     view; icmp slt/sle/sgt/sge from the signed-Order view. The 10
//     LangRef predicate names are the discriminant-projection.
//   Trigger: first consumer of comparison MEANING (cost model /
//     simplification / inhabitance dispatch — D2 pathway, std/integer
//     .dag Ord instances on signed/unsigned iN).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the (signedness,
//     ordering-cells) decomposition AT that consumer (a lens read over
//     `Ordering`), NOT a local match-over-predicate lookup; the LangRef
//     surface name stays the parse/emit-side authority.
type IntPredicate
  = IcmpEq | IcmpNe
  | IcmpUgt | IcmpUge | IcmpUlt | IcmpUle
  | IcmpSgt | IcmpSge | IcmpSlt | IcmpSle


// FloatPredicate — `fcmp` predicates (LangRef). Ordered/unordered
// variants exist because IEEE-754 NaN comparisons are unordered (the
// same reason `Float` is `ApproximateField`, not `Field`).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the `fcmp` consumer.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: an `{ ordered: Bool, … }` record makes
//      `fcmp true`/`fcmp false` (which have no ordering sense) carry a
//      meaningless `ordered` — illegal-state, P2; the named set is
//      faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: grounded in IEEE-754 ordering (the
//      ApproximateField rationale), but the predicate is the LangRef
//      surface token; faithfully the spec set, grounding via the float
//      inhabitance, not a duplicate authority.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an `fcmp` has EXACTLY ONE predicate.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: ordered/unordered/true/false do not
//      form one uniform `F<X>` projection over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef float-comparison predicate set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this projection set was correctly GREEN (pattern 3 already noted
// the IEEE-754 / std/float.dag ApproximateField grounding). Under the
// post-#3200 consumer-INDEPENDENT rule this is 🟡 YELLOW because a
// namable richer source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: {nan-policy ∈ Ordered | Unordered, op ∈ Eq | Order-
//     cell-set | True | False} over std/float.dag's `ApproximateField`
//     IEEE-754 comparison semantics. The 16 LangRef fcmp predicate
//     names (fcmp false/true + the 7 ordered + 7 unordered cells) are
//     the discriminant-projection of (nan-policy, ordering-cells).
//   Trigger: first consumer of fp-comparison MEANING (cost model /
//     simplification / NaN-aware lens — D2 pathway, std/float.dag
//     ApproximateField instance).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the (nan-policy,
//     ordering-cells) decomposition AT that consumer (a lens read over
//     `ApproximateField`'s comparison), NOT a local match-over-
//     predicate lookup; the LangRef surface name stays the parse/emit-
//     side authority.
type FloatPredicate
  = FcmpFalse
  | FcmpOeq | FcmpOgt | FcmpOge | FcmpOlt | FcmpOle | FcmpOne | FcmpOrd
  | FcmpUeq | FcmpUgt | FcmpUge | FcmpUlt | FcmpUle | FcmpUne | FcmpUno
  | FcmpTrue


// ConstExprOpcode — the constant-expression-admissible opcode subset
// (LangRef "Constant Expressions": a strict subset — no memory side
// effects). One `ConstantExpr` shape projects over this set.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the constexpr consumer;
//      shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — N/A dissolving: the promoted projected axis of
//      `ConstantExpr` (Practice-2 applied).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a constexpr opcode is the LangRef surface;
//      its numeric cases' algebra grounding is owned elsewhere (std/
//      scalar wave + pending D2, P2 — not declared here).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a constexpr has EXACTLY ONE opcode.
//   5. Parameterized family — APPLIED: the closed source set
//      `ConstantExpr` projects over (Practice 7); the dissolution target.
// Terminal: the LLVM-18.1.8 LangRef constant-expression-admissible
// opcode set, PINNED to that version. Authority = the spec itself
// (L-2), specifically
// https://releases.llvm.org/18.1.8/docs/LangRef.html#constant-expressions
// — membership is per that pinned LangRef, NOT model memory (memory is
// unreliable here: LangRef has reduced constexprs across versions, so
// the set is version-sensitive and must be read from the pinned spec).
// As of 18.1.8: `zext`/`sext` and the fp/div/select forms are gone, but
// `add`/`sub`/`mul`/`shl`/`xor`, `icmp`/`fcmp`, and `extractelement`/
// `insertelement`/`shufflevector` remain valid alongside the
// `trunc`/`ptrtoint`/`inttoptr`/`bitcast`/`addrspacecast`/
// `getelementptr` core. A constexpr opcode
// OUTSIDE this version-pinned set is the EXPLICIT
// `FidelityDisposition.FailClosed` boundary, the concrete
// machine-consumable `FidelityFeature.UnmodeledConstExpr`
// (`feature_disposition` maps it to `FailClosed`) — declared, never a
// silent fabricated parse or silent exclusion; a future LangRef revision that
// adds/removes one is an edit to THIS declaration against the pinned
// spec URL (the M3 spec-anchor analog of node.dag's C1 stop-signal).
type ConstExprOpcode
  = CeTrunc | CePtrToInt | CeIntToPtr | CeBitCast
  | CeAddrSpaceCast | CeGetElementPtr
  | CeAdd | CeSub | CeMul | CeShl | CeXor
  | CeICmp | CeFCmp
  | CeExtractElement | CeInsertElement | CeShuffleVector


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PhiIncoming — one (value, predecessor-block) pair of a phi node. A
// record (Conj), no ledger. `block` is a name-reference Symbol (K-1)
// resolved through the enclosing function's block namespace — the CFG
// predecessor edge is THIS structural pair, not a separate stored graph.
type PhiIncoming {
  value: LlvmValue
  block: Symbol
}


// PhiNode — an SSA φ. A record (Conj), no ledger. NOT a variant of
// `LlvmInstruction`: its block-start position is STRUCTURAL (it lives in
// `BasicBlock.phis`), so "a phi after a non-phi" is unrepresentable (P2)
// rather than a checker rule. Carries `result: Symbol` — the def-site
// name a φ defines; uses reference it via `LlvmValue.LocalRef` resolved
// through function scope (the A1 name-reference idiom). This is what
// makes loop-carried SSA representable: a φ's incoming may name an
// instruction whose operand names the φ — a finite cyclic def-use graph,
// not an infinite inlined tree. `result_type` is the load-bearing
// LangRef-typed result type.
type PhiNode {
  result: Symbol
  result_type: LlvmType
  incomings: List<PhiIncoming>
}


// SwitchCase — one (constant, target-block) arm of a `switch`. Record,
// no ledger.
type SwitchCase {
  match_value: LlvmConstant
  target: Symbol
}


// CallArgument — one actual argument of a `call`. Record, no ledger.
// (Parameter attributes that change ABI semantics — `byval`, `sret`,
// `signext` — are a declared C5 boundary: see `FidelityDisposition`;
// the structurally load-bearing operand is modeled here.)
type CallArgument {
  ty: LlvmType
  value: LlvmValue
}


// CallResult — a `call`/`invoke` either returns void (no def-site name,
// no result type) OR produces a value (a `result: Symbol` def-site name
// + result type). LangRef: `call void @f(...)` has NO `%x =`;
// `%r = call <ty> @f(...)` does. The earlier `Call { result: Symbol,
// result_type, … }` forced a result name onto EVERY call, making the
// LangRef-illegal "void call with a result name" representable
// (P2/extdeps-fidelity). Modeled as a typed two-case —
// the SAME alternative-forms-as-distinct-shapes discipline as
// `RetVoid`-vs-`RetValue` / `FunctionBody.Declared`-vs-`Defined`
// (never a sentinel/empty name by convention). Shared by `Call`
// (instruction) and `Invoke` (terminator) — both can be void or
// value-producing per LangRef (this also fills the prior gap where a
// value-producing `invoke`'s result was unmodeled).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by every call/invoke
//      consumer (emit, the SSA def-site resolver, cost); a shared
//      classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the reduction is a nullable/sentinel
//      result name ("" meaning void) — exactly the in-band-absence
//      anti-pattern INVARIANTS P2 + diagnostic.dag `Correction` forbid;
//      the typed two-case is faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a LangRef call-shape distinction, not an
//      algebra carrier; no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a call is void XOR value-producing, never
//      both — alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous shapes (nullary
//      vs name+type-carrying), not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the void-vs-value call-result partition; the typed sum makes
// "void call with a result name" structurally unrepresentable (P2).
type CallResult
  = VoidCall
  | ValueCall { name: Symbol, result_type: LlvmType }


// LandingPadCleanup — a `landingpad` either has the `cleanup` flag or it
// does not (LangRef "landingpad Instruction"). A genuine closed 2-valued
// LangRef distinction; a named sum, not a Bool (M4 — closed named
// distinction is an enum, not a boolean proxy; same rationale as
// VectorScaling/StructPacking).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the EH/unwind consumer
//      wherever a landingpad is lowered; not a per-consumer scattered
//      fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reduces only to a Bool — the M4
//      boolean-proxy for a closed named LangRef keyword.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef EH spelling distinction; no
//      richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a landingpad is cleanup XOR not; never both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two nullary spellings, not `F<X>`
//      over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed cleanup-vs-not LangRef landingpad distinction.
type LandingPadCleanup
  = IsCleanup
  | NotCleanup


// LandingClause — one clause of a `landingpad` (LangRef "landingpad
// Instruction"): `catch <type>` (a single type-info operand) or
// `filter <array-of-type>` (a list of type-infos). Two genuinely
// distinct LangRef clause forms with different payload shapes.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every landingpad consumer iterates the
//      clause list uniformly; not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: `CatchClause` carries ONE type,
//      `FilterClause` a type LIST — disjoint shapes; a flat
//      `{ kind, ty?, types? }` makes "a catch clause with a type list"
//      representable, the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef EH clause taxonomy; no
//      richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a clause is EXACTLY catch XOR filter; never
//      both — alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: a single type vs a type list are
//      not one `F<X>` over a declared set (heterogeneous arities).
// Terminal: the closed LangRef landingpad clause set.
type LandingClause
  = CatchClause { type_info: LlvmValue }
  | FilterClause { type_infos: List<LlvmValue> }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// AtomicOrdering — the CANONICAL FULL LangRef/C++11-C11 memory-model
// ordering set ("Atomic Memory Ordering Constraints"). This is the
// single-authority vocabulary of all six orderings; no instruction
// field carries it directly (each atomic op restricts it to its
// LangRef-valid subset — `Load/Store/Fence/AtomicRmw/CmpXchg*Ordering`,
// each declared as its own closed sum so an invalid (op, ordering) pair
// is structurally unrepresentable, P2). It is retained as the declared
// authority those subsets are documented against (the complete
// memory-model set is a real spec entity reviewers validate
// completeness against — not accidental dead code). The strength
// relation is a derived lens read over this order, NOT a stored field.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the ordering is read uniformly by every
//      atomic-op consumer (emit, the effect lens); shared, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a numeric "strength" proxy is the M4
//      stringly/number anti-pattern for a closed named memory-model set;
//      and acquire/release are NOT linearly comparable (acq_rel is not
//      "stronger acquire") — a scalar makes an illegal total order
//      representable, P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — the strength PARTIAL ORDER traces to lattice
//      theory, but the *constraint names* are the LangRef/C++-memory-
//      model surface; faithfully the spec set, its order a derived lens
//      read, not a stored authority.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an atomic op carries EXACTLY ONE ordering.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six are not `F<X>` over a
//      uniform declared set (the memory model is a partial order, not a
//      linear projection).
// Terminal: the closed LangRef/C++-memory-model ordering set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this was correctly GREEN (pattern 3 already noted the strength
// PARTIAL ORDER traces to lattice theory; the derived lens lives
// elsewhere). Under the post-#3200 consumer-INDEPENDENT rule this is
// 🟡 YELLOW because a namable richer source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: the C++11/C11 memory-model partial-order lattice
//     (Unordered ⊑ Monotonic ⊑ {Acquire, Release} ⊑ AcqRel ⊑ SeqCst —
//     the canonical happens-before structure). The six LangRef names
//     are the discriminant-projection of this lattice's elements; the
//     strength relation IS the lattice's partial order, not a separate
//     derivation. A future std/concurrency.dag would carry the lattice
//     authority; this enum becomes its surface labels.
//   Trigger: first consumer of ordering MEANING (effect lens / a
//     happens-before reasoning consumer / cost model — D2-adjacent
//     concurrency pathway).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the lattice-element
//     decomposition AT that consumer (a lens read over the partial
//     order), NOT a local match-over-ordering lookup; the LangRef
//     surface name stays the parse/emit-side authority.
type AtomicOrdering
  = OrderingUnordered
  | OrderingMonotonic
  | OrderingAcquire
  | OrderingRelease
  | OrderingAcqRel
  | OrderingSeqCst


// Per-OPERATION-valid atomic-ordering subsets (LangRef LLVM 18). The
// full `AtomicOrdering` is the memory-model authority; each atomic
// instruction restricts it, and — verified against LLVM 18 LangRef —
// every such restriction is a single closed SUBSET (no cross-field
// relation): `cmpxchg`'s historical "failure not stronger than success"
// rule was REMOVED in LLVM ≈3.9 and is NOT an LLVM-18 constraint
// (asserting it would be the ConstExprOpcode-class inverse error). So
// these are all STRUCTURALLY fixable with subset enums — illegal
// (op,ordering) pairs unrepresentable (P2 / modeling-discipline.md §2),
// the LoadOrdering/StoreOrdering precedent — NOT a 🟡 substrate-gap.
//   - CmpXchgSuccessOrdering: ≥ monotonic (not unordered).
//   - CmpXchgFailureOrdering: ≥ monotonic, NOT release/acq_rel.
//   - FenceOrdering: ∈ {acquire, release, acq_rel, seq_cst} (a fence is
//     not unordered/monotonic).
//   - AtomicRmwOrdering: not unordered.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) — all
// four 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger (applies to each) — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the ordering is read uniformly by the
//      atomic-op lowering + the effect lens; a shared classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a numeric "strength" proxy is the M4
//      anti-pattern and acquire/release are not linearly comparable
//      (same rationale as `AtomicOrdering`); the named per-op subset is
//      faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the constraint names are the LangRef/
//      C++-memory-model surface; the per-op valid subset is the spec's
//      restriction, not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an op carries EXACTLY ONE ordering;
//      alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: not one `F<X>` over a declared set
//      (the memory model is a partial order; each is a spec-defined
//      per-op restriction, not a mechanical projection).
// Terminal: the closed LangRef-18 per-operation-valid ordering subsets;
// invalid (operation, ordering) pairs structurally unrepresentable (P2).
type CmpXchgSuccessOrdering
  = SuccessMonotonic
  | SuccessAcquire
  | SuccessRelease
  | SuccessAcqRel
  | SuccessSeqCst

type CmpXchgFailureOrdering
  = FailureMonotonic
  | FailureAcquire
  | FailureSeqCst

type FenceOrdering
  = FenceAcquire
  | FenceRelease
  | FenceAcqRel
  | FenceSeqCst

type AtomicRmwOrdering
  = RmwOrdMonotonic
  | RmwOrdAcquire
  | RmwOrdRelease
  | RmwOrdAcqRel
  | RmwOrdSeqCst


// AtomicRmwOp — the `atomicrmw` sub-operation (LangRef). One
// `AtomicRmw` instruction projects over this closed set (Practice 7).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the atomicrmw consumer.
//   2. Variant-is-data — N/A dissolving: the promoted projected axis.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: xchg/add/and/or/xor/max/min trace to
//      the `iN` ring/lattice instances, grounded THERE; the opcode is
//      the LangRef surface token, not a duplicate authority.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an atomicrmw has EXACTLY ONE sub-op.
//   5. Parameterized family — APPLIED: the closed source set the single
//      `AtomicRmw` shape projects over (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the LLVM-18 LangRef `atomicrmw` sub-operation set, COMPLETE
// for LLVM 18 (incl. `fmax`/`fmin`/`uinc_wrap`/`udec_wrap` — added here
// as the spec-faithfulness fix; earlier cuts omitted them). A future
// LangRef revision adding a sub-op is an edit to THIS declaration (the
// M3 spec-anchor analog of node.dag's C1 stop-signal).
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this projection set was correctly GREEN (pattern 3 already noted
// xchg/add/and/or/xor/max/min trace to the `iN` ring/lattice instances,
// "grounded THERE"). Under the post-#3200 consumer-INDEPENDENT rule
// this is 🟡 YELLOW because a namable richer source EXISTS:
//   Richer source: each LLVM atomicrmw sub-op names ONE algebra
//     operation on the operand-type's inhabitance. The richer source
//     SPLITS into two distinct facts per M9/INVARIANTS P1 — (a) the
//     landed std/algebra.dag STRUCTURE ONTOLOGY the sub-op would
//     inhabit, and (b) the per-(type, op) WITNESS, which may or may
//     not yet be landed at the inhabitant. Conflating the two is the
//     trap (briansrls + codex BLOCKING caught it).
//     Landed-witness inventory on origin/main matches BinaryOpcode's
//     (FIVE total: int_abelian_group/integer.dag:234,
//     int_ordered_ring/integer.dag:244,
//     bool_boolean_algebra/logic.dag:120,
//     nat_additive_commutative_monoid/nat.dag:151,
//     nat_semiring/nat.dag:174). int_abelian_group is the `add:`
//     sub-component OF int_ordered_ring (one Int-arithmetic story);
//     the Nat witnesses are NOT a richer source for LLVM iN
//     (iN ≅ Z/2^N ≠ ℕ).
//     (a) LANDED ONTOLOGY (the structure types exist on origin/main):
//       - RmwAdd → Semiring (std/algebra.dag:185); ontology landed;
//         abstract-Int story rides int_ordered_ring's `add:` /
//         `mul:` sub-components; per-iN per-width witnesses deferred
//         to D2 fan-out.
//       - RmwSub → Ring (std/algebra.dag:197 — additive inverse,
//         which Semiring lacks); ontology landed; abstract-Int story
//         rides int_abelian_group. Per-iN per-width witnesses
//         deferred to D2 fan-out.
//       - RmwAnd / RmwOr / RmwXor / RmwNand → BooleanAlgebra (std/
//         algebra.dag:291); nand is De-Morgan over the same carrier
//         (NOT a fresh algebra). Landed data witness on origin/main =
//         `bool_boolean_algebra: BooleanAlgebra<Bool>` (std/logic.dag:
//         120). Per-iN-bitwise inhabitance deferred to D2 fan-out.
//       - RmwFAdd / RmwFSub → ApproximateField (std/algebra.dag:257);
//         machine-readable per-Float witness EXPLICITLY WITHHELD on
//         origin/main per std/float.dag DECISIONS.md D3 / INVARIANTS
//         P1/P3 (std/float.dag:136). The ontology binding holds; the
//         witness lands in a later std/float.dag phase.
//     (b) NAMED-MISSING CARRIERS (no landed std/algebra.dag ontology
//     match — future authority + trigger named here):
//       - RmwXchg → atomic-swap-with-prior-value-return primitive.
//         NOT a Monoid (second-projection has no identity element —
//         there is no `e` such that `swap(x, e) == x`; a "last write
//         wins" framing is not a two-sided identity Monoid op).
//         MISSING future authority = std/concurrency.dag atomic-swap
//         primitive (carries the prior-value-return semantics);
//         trigger = first consumer of atomic-swap MEANING.
//       - RmwMax / RmwMin (signed-Ord) and RmwUMax / RmwUMin
//         (unsigned-Ord) → integer max/min over the corresponding Ord
//         inhabitance. std/algebra.dag has Lattice (271), but a
//         Lattice on iN-as-numbers (not iN-as-bitwise-BoolN) needs
//         the signed-vs-unsigned Ord inhabitance to derive
//         meet=min/join=max — that derivation is a future bridge.
//         MISSING future authority = std/integer.dag (signed-Ord-max /
//         unsigned-Ord-min) Lattice instance; trigger = first consumer
//         of integer-max/min MEANING.
//       - RmwFMax / RmwFMin → IEEE-754 float max/min with NaN policy.
//         ApproximateField has NO lattice operation, AND the NaN-
//         propagation policy is a IEEE-754-specific semantic distinct
//         from algebraic max/min. MISSING future authority = std/
//         float.dag IEEE-754-max/min op (with documented NaN policy);
//         trigger = first consumer of float-max/min MEANING.
//       - RmwUIncWrap / RmwUDecWrap → bounded-counter primitive
//         (wrap-on-bound, compare-and-conditionally-{inc,dec}). No
//         landed carrier. MISSING future authority = std/integer.dag
//         bounded-counter carrier; trigger = first consumer of
//         wrap-counter MEANING.
//   Trigger: first consumer of atomicrmw MEANING (effect lens / cost
//     model / inhabitance dispatch — D2 pathway).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the (algebra-carrier,
//     algebra-op) mapping AT that consumer (a lens read), NOT a local
//     match-over-sub-op lookup; the LangRef surface name stays the
//     parse/emit-side authority.
type AtomicRmwOp
  = RmwXchg | RmwAdd | RmwSub | RmwAnd | RmwNand | RmwOr | RmwXor
  | RmwMax | RmwMin | RmwUMax | RmwUMin | RmwFAdd | RmwFSub
  | RmwFMax | RmwFMin | RmwUIncWrap | RmwUDecWrap


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LlvmInstruction — the non-terminator, non-phi instruction set (LangRef
// "Instruction Reference", minus terminators (`Terminator`) and phi
// (`PhiNode`, structurally hoisted to the block)). Practice-7
// projections (`Binary`/`Conversion`/`AtomicRmw`) collapse the
// mechanically-uniform families to one shape over a closed opcode set;
// the genuinely structurally-distinct instructions stay faithful
// variants.
//
// `result: Symbol` — the SSA def-site NAME. A value-producing
// instruction carries the K-1-opaque `Symbol` it defines (`%x`/`%1`);
// uses are NAME-REFERENCES (`LlvmValue.LocalRef`) resolved through
// function scope — node.dag A1's cross-declaration idiom (precedent:
// diagnostic.dag `reason: Symbol`). This is the FINITE, A1-faithful
// REALIZATION of the value-graph intent (operands reference their
// producers): the def-use graph may be CYCLIC (every loop has a
// loop-carried phi referencing an instruction that references the phi),
// and a name-reference keeps the bounded Node tree FINITE while
// expressing that cycle — exactly why A1 mandates name-reference
// recursion. (An earlier cut INLINED the producer node; that could not
// finitely represent cyclic loop-carried SSA — a P1/M3 spec-narrowing
// excluding ALL loops — and was the A1 violation A1 exists to prevent.
// `result: Symbol` is a declaration FIELD = a name-reference, NOT a
// runtime Symbol mint; the K-1 no-mint rule was specific to the removed
// inhabitance lambdas, not declaration-class fields.) The textual `%x`
// spelling is `DeclaredNormalized` (α-equivalent — `Symbol` is opaque,
// so the content-hash is rename-invariant). `result_type` IS
// load-bearing (LLVM is typed). `Store`/`Fence` produce NO value and
// carry no `result`/`result_type` — modeled by the absence of those
// fields, not a nullable result (P2 — "a store with a result" is
// unrepresentable).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every instruction is dispatched on by
//      the emit/cost/effect/SSA consumers; it is THE shared classifier
//      of the computation surface, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — PARTIALLY APPLIED then FAILS to fully dissolve:
//      the mechanically-uniform families ARE folded to data
//      (`Binary`/`Conversion`/`AtomicRmw` over opcode sets — Practice-2/7
//      done). The residual variants (Load/Store/GEP/Select/Call/…) have
//      genuinely disjoint shapes (Store { value, ptr }; Call { callee,
//      args }); a flat record makes "a Store with call arguments"
//      representable — the inverse of P2. Cannot fully fold.
//   3. Algebraic form — arithmetic opcodes DO trace to the abstract
//      algebra, but that grounding is OWNED ELSEWHERE (std/ scalar wave
//      + pending D2, P2 — not declared here); the instruction is the
//      bidirectional grammar surface the model needs — two faithful
//      facts in two owners, not duplication.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an instruction is EXACTLY ONE kind; never a
//      coordinate tuple of all kinds.
//   5. Parameterized family — APPLIED to the uniform sub-families
//      (the opcode-set projections) and FAILS for the residual: Load/
//      Store/GEP/Select/Call/… are not one `F<X>` over a single declared
//      set (heterogeneous arities and operand roles).
// Terminal: the MODELED LangRef (LLVM 18) non-terminator non-phi
// instruction set — the arithmetic / bitwise / conversion / comparison /
// memory / aggregate / vector / EH-pad / call / freeze / fneg / va_arg
// core, maximally Practice-7-collapsed; residual variants irreducibly
// distinct. This is NOT a claim that EVERY LangRef opcode is enumerated
// here: a LangRef instruction outside the modeled core (a target-
// specific / deprecated / newly-added LangRef opcode) is the EXPLICIT
// `FidelityDisposition.FailClosed` boundary, the concrete
// machine-consumable `FidelityFeature.UnmodeledInstruction`
// (`feature_disposition` maps it to `FailClosed`) — declared,
// diagnosed, never silently dropped or fabricated (the no-engine
// discipline; the honest
// reconciliation of "modeled spec core" vs "the full LangRef surface" —
// completeness of F is declared, not over-claimed). Extending the
// modeled core is an upstream-LangRef-driven edit to THIS declaration
// (the M3 spec-anchor analog of node.dag's C1 stop-signal).
type LlvmInstruction
  = Binary {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      op: BinaryOpcode
      lhs: LlvmValue
      rhs: LlvmValue
    }
  | Conversion {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      op: ConversionOpcode
      value: LlvmValue
    }
  | IntCompare {
      result: Symbol
      predicate: IntPredicate
      lhs: LlvmValue
      rhs: LlvmValue
    }
  | FloatCompare {
      result: Symbol
      predicate: FloatPredicate
      lhs: LlvmValue
      rhs: LlvmValue
    }
  | Alloca {
      result: Symbol
      allocated_type: LlvmType
      element_count: LlvmValue
    }
  | Load {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      pointer: LlvmValue
      ordering: LoadAtomicity
    }
  | Store {
      value: LlvmValue
      pointer: LlvmValue
      ordering: StoreAtomicity
    }
  | GetElementPtr {
      result: Symbol
      source_type: LlvmType
      base: LlvmValue
      indices: List<LlvmValue>
    }
  | AtomicRmw {
      result: Symbol
      op: AtomicRmwOp
      pointer: LlvmValue
      operand: LlvmValue
      ordering: AtomicRmwOrdering
    }
  | CmpXchg {
      result: Symbol
      pointer: LlvmValue
      compare_value: LlvmValue
      new_value: LlvmValue
      success_ordering: CmpXchgSuccessOrdering
      failure_ordering: CmpXchgFailureOrdering
    }
  | Fence {
      ordering: FenceOrdering
    }
  | ExtractValue {
      result: Symbol
      aggregate: LlvmValue
      indices: List<Int>
    }
  | InsertValue {
      result: Symbol
      aggregate: LlvmValue
      element: LlvmValue
      indices: List<Int>
    }
  | ExtractElement {
      result: Symbol
      vector: LlvmValue
      index: LlvmValue
    }
  | InsertElement {
      result: Symbol
      vector: LlvmValue
      element: LlvmValue
      index: LlvmValue
    }
  | ShuffleVector {
      result: Symbol
      first: LlvmValue
      second: LlvmValue
      mask: LlvmValue
    }
  | Select {
      result: Symbol
      condition: LlvmValue
      if_true: LlvmValue
      if_false: LlvmValue
    }
  | Call {
      callee: LlvmValue
      arguments: List<CallArgument>
      result: CallResult
    }
  | Freeze {
      result: Symbol
      value: LlvmValue
    }
  | FNeg {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      operand: LlvmValue
    }
  | VaArg {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      va_list: LlvmValue
      arg_type: LlvmType
    }
  | LandingPad {
      result: Symbol
      result_type: LlvmType
      is_cleanup: LandingPadCleanup
      clauses: List<LandingClause>
    }
  | CatchPad {
      result: Symbol
      catch_switch: LlvmValue
      args: List<LlvmValue>
    }
  | CleanupPad {
      result: Symbol
      parent_pad: LlvmValue
      args: List<LlvmValue>
    }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// OPERATION-SPECIFIC OPERAND CONSTRAINTS — consolidated TRACKED 🟡
// SCAFFOLD (the merged diagnostic.dag `Extent.ByteRange` three-bridge
// precedent — a CHECKABLE trigger, not a prose-only disposition).
//
// Several instructions carry LangRef operand-relation constraints that
// the broad `LlvmValue` / `LlvmType` carriers do NOT type-enforce, e.g.:
//   - `icmp`/`fcmp`: both operands same type; `icmp` int/ptr, `fcmp`
//     float (or same-shaped vector); result is `i1`/vector-of-`i1`.
//   - `getelementptr`: indices are integer-typed; struct indices are
//     `i32` constants; result type is the GEP-walked type.
//   - `select`: condition is `i1` (or vector-of-`i1` matching operands);
//     the two arms share the result type.
//   - `Binary`: `lhs`/`rhs`/result share one type; float opcodes need
//     float operands, integer opcodes integer operands.
//   - `insert/extractelement`, `shufflevector`: element/index/mask type
//     and length relations.
// (Atomic-ordering op-specific validity is NO LONGER in this residual:
// Load/Store/Fence/AtomicRmw/CmpXchg orderings were ALL structurally
// fixed via per-operation subset enums — `LoadOrdering`/`StoreOrdering`/
// `FenceOrdering`/`AtomicRmwOrdering`/`CmpXchgSuccessOrdering`/
// `CmpXchgFailureOrdering`. Verified against LLVM 18: the historical
// `cmpxchg` "failure not stronger than success" cross-field rule was
// REMOVED in LLVM ≈3.9, so NO cross-field atomic-ordering refinement
// remains — all are single-subset, structurally unrepresentable-when-
// invalid, not a substrate-gap.)
// These are NOT collapsed-away facts — they are real LangRef invariants
// the model currently leaves PRODUCER-SIDE because the substrate to
// type-encode an operand-relation refinement (a dependent/refined
// typed-value carrier) is std/cardinality.dag's refinement substrate,
// which is parallel Wave-A1 and NOT in this file's whitelist.
// Introducing a constrained carrier here would be exactly the
// improvise-past-not-ready-substrate failure mode; the sanctioned path
// (operator-ratified, merged in diagnostic.dag) is the tracked scaffold
// with the three bridge properties:
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the broad operands are provisional,
//       not the terminal operand-relation-refined shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only LangRef-valid
//       operand-type relations (same-type binary/compare; i1 select
//       cond; integer GEP indices; …). Until the substrate enforces it
//       the invariant is producer-side; a violating instruction is a
//       producer bug, not a legal value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — CHECKABLE, not prose: when
//       std/cardinality.dag's refinement substrate lands (its own T-3
//       entry — a concrete, observable file/feature-landing event, the
//       SAME trigger the merged diagnostic.dag `Extent.ByteRange` 🟡
//       uses), these operand relations refine to type-enforced
//       constraints and this residual reclassifies 🟢. The trigger is a
//       substrate-landing fact a reviewer can check, not a vague
//       "later".
// This converts root-cause #3's "broad carriers + prose-only
// dispositions" into a single tracked residual with a checkable trigger,
// without improvising refinement substrate that is not yet ratified.


// LoadOrdering / StoreOrdering — the LangRef per-DIRECTION-valid atomic
// memory-ordering subsets. LangRef forbids `release`/`acq_rel` on an
// atomic LOAD and `acquire`/`acq_rel` on an atomic STORE; reusing the
// full `AtomicOrdering` for both made those invalid accesses
// representable. The fix is STRUCTURAL, no new
// substrate: two distinct closed subset enums so an invalid
// direction/ordering pair is UNREPRESENTABLE (P2 / modeling-discipline.md
// §2 — illegal states unrepresentable), the same discipline as
// ConditionalBr-vs-Br and FunctionBody entry/rest. The full six-valued
// `AtomicOrdering` remains the authority for Fence/AtomicRmw/CmpXchg
// (their own op-specific validity is the consolidated 🟡 note above).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) — both
// enums 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger (applies to each) — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the ordering is read uniformly by the
//      atomic-load/store lowering + the effect lens; a shared
//      classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a numeric "strength" proxy is the M4
//      anti-pattern AND acquire/release are not linearly comparable
//      (same rationale as `AtomicOrdering`); the named per-direction
//      subset is the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: the constraint NAMES are the LangRef/
//      C++-memory-model surface; the per-direction valid subset is the
//      spec's direction restriction, not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a load/store carries EXACTLY ONE ordering;
//      alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: not one `F<X>` over a declared set
//      (the memory model is a partial order; the per-direction subset is
//      a spec-defined restriction, not a mechanical projection).
// Terminal: the closed LangRef per-direction-valid ordering subsets
// (load excludes release/acq_rel; store excludes acquire/acq_rel) —
// invalid direction/ordering pairs structurally unrepresentable (P2),
// which is exactly why these are distinct from the full `AtomicOrdering`.
type LoadOrdering
  = LoadUnordered
  | LoadMonotonic
  | LoadAcquire
  | LoadSeqCst

type StoreOrdering
  = StoreUnordered
  | StoreMonotonic
  | StoreRelease
  | StoreSeqCst


// LoadAtomicity / StoreAtomicity — a load/store is either plain or
// atomic-with-a-DIRECTION-valid-ordering. Modeled as a sum (NOT an
// optional ordering) so the diagnostic.dag/Correction discipline holds:
// "plain" is a structural case, never a bare absence; a plain access
// carrying an ordering, or an atomic access with none, is
// unrepresentable (P2).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) — both
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger (applies to each) — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly wherever a load/store is
//      lowered or its effects analyzed; not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the "data" reduction is exactly
//      `Option<{Load,Store}Ordering>`, the bare-`None`-by-convention
//      shape INVARIANTS P2 + the diagnostic.dag `Correction` forbid.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a LangRef access-mode distinction, not an
//      algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an access is plain XOR atomic, never both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous shapes (nullary
//      vs ordering-carrying), not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the plain-vs-atomic access partition (per direction); the
// typed-sum is the dissolved shape (an `Option` would be the smell).
type LoadAtomicity
  = PlainLoad
  | AtomicLoad { ordering: LoadOrdering }

type StoreAtomicity
  = PlainStore
  | AtomicStore { ordering: StoreOrdering }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Terminator — the single block-ending instruction (LangRef "Terminator
// Instructions"). A basic block has EXACTLY ONE; modeled as a distinct
// type (not an `LlvmInstruction` variant) so "a block ending in `add`"
// or "a `ret` mid-block" is structurally unrepresentable — the
// BasicBlock record (below) carries body and terminator separately, the
// P2 illegal-state-unrepresentable discipline.
//
//   - RetVoid / RetValue { value } — `ret void` vs `ret <ty> <v>`, two
//     distinct LangRef forms (NOT `Option<value>` — the Correction
//     precedent: a typed two-case, never a bare absence).
//   - UnconditionalBr { target } / ConditionalBr { condition, if_true,
//     if_false } — `br label` vs `br i1, label, label`, two distinct
//     LangRef forms (NOT a Bool + optional targets — illegal "conditional
//     branch with no condition" unrepresentable, P2).
//   - Switch { value, default_target, cases }.
//   - IndirectBr { address, possible_targets }.
//   - Unreachable.
//   - Invoke / CallBr / Resume / CatchSwitch / CleanupRet / CatchRet —
//     the exception/funclet terminators (LangRef "Exception Handling").
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the terminator IS the CFG-edge authority
//      every control-flow / dominance / effect consumer reads; shared,
//      not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: disjoint shapes (RetVoid nullary;
//      Switch { value, default, cases }; ConditionalBr { cond, t, f }).
//      A flat record makes "Unreachable with switch cases"
//      representable — the inverse of P2.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a terminator is a CFG-edge constructor, not
//      an algebra operation on a carrier; no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a block has EXACTLY ONE terminator kind;
//      genuine alternatives, never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the terminators are structurally
//      heterogeneous (0–N successor edges, distinct operand roles); NOT
//      one `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef terminator set; the CFG edges fall out of
// THIS structure (see `block_successors`), dominance is a lens read over
// it — never a stored field.
type Terminator
  = RetVoid
  | RetValue { value: LlvmValue }
  | UnconditionalBr { target: Symbol }
  | ConditionalBr { condition: LlvmValue, if_true: Symbol, if_false: Symbol }
  | Switch { value: LlvmValue, default_target: Symbol, cases: List<SwitchCase> }
  | IndirectBr { address: LlvmValue, possible_targets: List<Symbol> }
  | Unreachable
  | Invoke {
      callee: LlvmValue
      arguments: List<CallArgument>
      result: CallResult
      normal_target: Symbol
      unwind_target: Symbol
    }
  | CallBr {
      callee: LlvmValue
      arguments: List<CallArgument>
      result: CallResult
      fallthrough: Symbol
      indirect_targets: List<Symbol>
    }
  | Resume { value: LlvmValue }
  | CatchSwitch { result: Symbol, parent_pad: LlvmValue, handlers: List<Symbol>, unwind: UnwindDest }
  | CleanupRet { cleanup_pad: LlvmValue, unwind: UnwindDest }
  | CatchRet { catch_pad: LlvmValue, successor: Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// UnwindDest — the unwind destination of an EH terminator. LangRef
// `catchswitch`/`cleanupret` each allow EITHER `unwind label <bb>` OR
// `unwind to caller` — two genuinely distinct legal forms. The earlier
// `unwind_target: Symbol` forced a block label, making the LangRef-legal
// `unwind to caller` UNREPRESENTABLE (a P1/M3 spec-narrowing: valid
// source programs not modelable). Fixed as a
// typed two-case sum, the SAME alternative-forms-as-distinct-shapes
// discipline as `RetVoid`-vs-`RetValue` / `UnconditionalBr`-vs-
// `ConditionalBr` (a typed case, NEVER an in-band sentinel label). Only
// `catchswitch`/`cleanupret` carry it — `invoke`/`catchret` unwind/jump
// is ALWAYS a label per LangRef (verified — no inverse over-application).
// (The companion `catchswitch within none` form is NOT a narrowing: the
// parent operand is a token VALUE in LLVM's own model — `within none`
// is faithfully `parent_pad = ConstantOperand { constant: NoneToken }`,
// `within %pad` is the pad's token result; both representable.)
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the CFG/unwind
//      consumer (`block_successors`, the effect lens); a shared
//      classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the reduction is a nullable/sentinel
//      label (`unwind_target` = "" meaning to-caller) — exactly the
//      in-band-absence anti-pattern INVARIANTS P2 + diagnostic.dag
//      `Correction` forbid; the typed two-case is faithful.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a LangRef EH control-flow form, not an
//      algebra carrier; no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: an unwind is EXACTLY to-caller XOR
//      to-a-label; never both — alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous shapes (nullary
//      vs label-carrying), not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef unwind-destination partition; the typed
// sum makes `unwind to caller` representable WITHOUT a sentinel label
// (P1/M3 faithful — neither over- nor under-constrained).
type UnwindDest
  = UnwindToCaller
  | UnwindToLabel { target: Symbol }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BasicBlock — a label + the SSA-mandated structure: phi nodes FIRST,
// then ordinary instructions, then EXACTLY ONE terminator. Record (Conj),
// no ledger. The phi-before-body LangRef rule is STRUCTURAL here (a
// separate `phis` field of `PhiNode`) — "a phi after a non-phi
// instruction" is unrepresentable, not a checker rule (P2 / modeling-
// discipline.md §2, the std/node.dag EdgeLabel no-stored-index
// discipline applied to block structure). Block textual order in a
// function is NOT semantic (the CFG defines order); the entry block is
// the explicit required `FunctionBody.Defined.entry` field — a named
// distinguished block, NOT first-of-a-list by convention (so an
// entry-less definition is structurally unrepresentable, P2); no
// `is_entry` flag.
type BasicBlock {
  label: Symbol
  phis: List<PhiNode>
  body: List<LlvmInstruction>
  terminator: Terminator
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Linkage — symbol linkage (LangRef "Linkage Types"). Closed set.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟡 YELLOW (deferred-on-consumer; re-scoped under #3200 rule-change
// 2026-05-16 — namable richer source exists; see #3200 RE-SCOPE
// footer before the type declaration below). Ledger — five patterns
// attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: linkage is read uniformly by emit/the
//      symbol-resolution model; not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reduces to an opaque keyword string —
//      the M4 stringly anti-pattern for a closed named LangRef set.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef linkage taxonomy; no richer
//      std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a symbol has EXACTLY ONE linkage.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the linkages are heterogeneous
//      keyword spellings, not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed LangRef linkage set.
//
// #3200 RE-SCOPE (rule-change 2026-05-16, docs/modeling-discipline.md
// merged on origin/main): under the pre-#3200 consumer-DEPENDENT GREEN
// rule this was correctly GREEN. Under the post-#3200 consumer-
// INDEPENDENT rule this is 🟡 YELLOW because a namable richer source
// EXISTS:
//   Richer source: a multi-axis classifier {visibility ∈ Private |
//     Internal | External | AvailableExternally, mergeability ∈ Unique
//     | LinkOnce | Weak | Common | Appending, odr-strictness ∈ NonOdr |
//     Odr, weakness ∈ Strong | Weak} over a future std/symbol.dag /
//     linkage authority (the same partition C++/ELF/COFF rest on). The
//     11 LangRef linkage names are the discriminant-projection of
//     valid (visibility, mergeability, odr, weakness) cells.
//   Trigger: first consumer of linkage MEANING (symbol-resolution lens
//     / cross-TU dedup model / a future std/symbol.dag visibility
//     reasoner — link-time pathway).
//   Obligation: the first meaning-consumer owes the multi-axis
//     decomposition AT that consumer (a lens read), NOT a local
//     match-over-linkage lookup; the LangRef keyword stays the
//     parse/emit-side authority.
type Linkage
  = LinkagePrivate
  | LinkageInternal
  | LinkageAvailableExternally
  | LinkageLinkOnce
  | LinkageLinkOnceOdr
  | LinkageWeak
  | LinkageWeakOdr
  | LinkageCommon
  | LinkageAppending
  | LinkageExternal
  | LinkageExternalWeak


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FunctionBody — a function is a declaration (no blocks) OR a definition
// with AT LEAST ONE block whose first is the entry. Modeled as a sum,
// and the definition arm is `entry: BasicBlock, rest: List<BasicBlock>`
// — NOT `blocks: List<BasicBlock>`. The split makes the LangRef
// "a definition has ≥1 block, control enters the first" invariant
// STRUCTURAL: "a definition with no entry block" (an empty block list)
// is now UNREPRESENTABLE (P2 / modeling-discipline.md §2 — illegal
// states unrepresentable), not merely asserted in prose. `entry` is the
// distinguished entry block (no `is_entry` flag, no first-of-list
// convention — the entry IS a named required field); `rest` is the
// remaining blocks, whose textual order is not semantic (the CFG defines
// order). This is the same illegal-state-unrepresentable discipline as
// std/node.dag's EdgeLabel no-stored-index and diagnostic.dag's
// `Correction` (a typed shape, never a bare absence / empty-list-by-
// convention). (A plain `List<BasicBlock>` would admit the empty list —
// an entry-less definition — contradicting this note; the entry/rest
// split excludes it structurally.)
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every function consumer (emit, the call
//      graph, cost) branches declaration-vs-definition uniformly.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the "data" reduction is an empty-vs-
//      nonempty `List<BasicBlock>` standing in for the distinction —
//      exactly the convention-enforced absence INVARIANTS P2 and the
//      Correction precedent forbid (and which the `entry`/`rest` split
//      now structurally excludes).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a function is declared XOR defined, never
//      both.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous shapes (nullary
//      vs entry+rest-carrying), not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the declared-vs-defined partition; the typed-sum with an
// explicit required `entry` is the dissolved form (an empty-list
// convention would be the smell — now structurally impossible).
type FunctionBody
  = Declared
  | Defined { entry: BasicBlock, rest: List<BasicBlock> }


// FunctionParameter — one formal parameter: its type and SSA name.
// Record, no ledger. (ABI-affecting parameter attributes are a declared
// C5 boundary — see `FidelityDisposition`.)
type FunctionParameter {
  ty: LlvmType
  name: Symbol
}


// LlvmFunction — a module-level function (LangRef "Functions"). Record,
// no ledger. `name` is the global symbol; the body sum carries the
// declaration/definition distinction structurally.
type LlvmFunction {
  name: Symbol
  result_type: LlvmType
  parameters: List<FunctionParameter>
  var_arg: VarArg
  linkage: Linkage
  body: FunctionBody
}


// GlobalMutability — `@g = global …` (mutable) vs `@g = constant …`
// (read-only). A genuine 2-valued LangRef distinction; a named sum, not
// a Bool (M4 — closed named distinction is an enum, not a boolean proxy).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by emit / the
//      const-propagation effect read; not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: reduces only to a Bool — the M4
//      boolean-proxy for a closed named LangRef keyword.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a leaf LangRef spelling distinction.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a global is mutable XOR constant.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two nullary spellings, not `F<X>`.
// Terminal: the closed mutable-vs-constant LangRef distinction.
type GlobalMutability
  = MutableGlobal
  | ConstantGlobal


// GlobalInitializer — a global is either externally-initialized (a
// declaration) or carries a constant initializer. Sum, not optional
// (the Correction / FunctionBody discipline).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by emit / the linker
//      model; not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the reduction is `Option<LlvmConstant>`
//      — the bare-absence-by-convention shape P2 / Correction forbid.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: not an algebra carrier.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a global is external XOR initialized.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: two heterogeneous shapes (nullary
//      vs constant-carrying), not `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the external-vs-initialized partition; typed-sum dissolved.
type GlobalInitializer
  = ExternalInit
  | InitializedWith { constant: LlvmConstant }


// LlvmGlobal — a module-level global variable (LangRef "Global
// Variables"). Record, no ledger.
type LlvmGlobal {
  name: Symbol
  value_type: LlvmType
  mutability: GlobalMutability
  linkage: Linkage
  initializer: GlobalInitializer
  address_space: Int
}


// NamedStructDef — a module-level identified struct (`%T = type { … }`).
// Record, no ledger. Recursion through `%T` is the `NamedType { name }`
// reference resolved here through the module namespace (node.dag A1
// name-reference recursion — NOT a self-referential type definition).
type NamedStructDef {
  name: Symbol
  definition: LlvmType
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// LlvmModule — the translation unit (LangRef "Module Structure"). Record,
// no ledger.
//
// `data_layout` and `target_triple` are opaque `Symbol`s (K-1): they are
// target-configuration TOKENS the Node graph never branches on — their
// internal grammar is a target concern, not IR semantics. They are a
// DECLARED-NORMALIZED C5 boundary (preserved verbatim through
// emit∘ingest as an opaque token, never re-spelled or guessed) — see
// `FidelityDisposition`. Modeling the datalayout sub-grammar would be
// out-of-scope spec inflation (M3 — model the IR, not every target
// triple); declaring it opaque is the honest boundary, not a fabrication.
type LlvmModule {
  data_layout: Symbol
  target_triple: Symbol
  named_structs: List<NamedStructDef>
  globals: List<LlvmGlobal>
  functions: List<LlvmFunction>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// SSA / CFG / dominance — STRUCTURAL, not stored (the B2-OMNI probe made
// concrete). `block_successors` derives a block's CFG out-edges purely
// from its terminator — proving the CFG is plain Node structure, not a
// separate stored graph (INVARIANTS P2 single-authority). Dominance
// ("every definition dominates all its uses", the SSA well-formedness
// law) is then a LENS read over this derived CFG + the def-site Node
// graph — the same shape as the complexity/effect lenses (lens/*), never
// a `dominates` field here (feedback_no_annotations: a stored dominator
// edge would be a second authority for what the CFG already determines).
// One self-recursion-free fold, terminating by structural list descent
// (std/node.dag A2 implicit case).
//
// `unwind_successors` projects an `UnwindDest` to its CFG edge(s):
// `unwind to caller` adds NO intra-function successor (control leaves
// the function), `unwind label %bb` adds that one edge — the structural
// reason `UnwindDest` is a typed sum, not a sentinel label.
fn unwind_successors(d: UnwindDest) -> List<Symbol> {
  match d {
    UnwindToCaller => []
    UnwindToLabel { target: t } => [t]
  }
}

fn block_successors(t: Terminator) -> List<Symbol> {
  match t {
    RetVoid => []
    RetValue { value: _ } => []
    UnconditionalBr { target: tgt } => [tgt]
    ConditionalBr { condition: _, if_true: a, if_false: b } => [a, b]
    Switch { value: _, default_target: d, cases: cs } =>
      concat([d], map(cs, fn(c) { c.target }))
    IndirectBr { address: _, possible_targets: ts } => ts
    Unreachable => []
    Invoke { callee: _, arguments: _, result: _, normal_target: n, unwind_target: u } => [n, u]
    CallBr { callee: _, arguments: _, result: _, fallthrough: f, indirect_targets: it } =>
      concat([f], it)
    Resume { value: _ } => []
    CatchSwitch { result: _, parent_pad: _, handlers: hs, unwind: u } => concat(hs, unwind_successors(d: u))
    CleanupRet { cleanup_pad: _, unwind: u } => unwind_successors(d: u)
    CatchRet { catch_pad: _, successor: s } => [s]
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// block_well_formed — a CHECKED structural invariant, resolved per the
// operator-ratified directive **D3** (std/node.dag header, "operator
// directive D3, 2026-05-15": a structural rule whose type-unrepresentable
// form would require a core-type redesign is CHECK-enforced via a
// well-formed predicate — "keep one shape; the illegal state is
// CHECK-enforced, not type-unrepresentable" — NOT a shape redesign;
// node.dag's own `node_well_formed` is exactly this pattern for the
// edge-discipline rule).
//
// Applied here (NO `BasicBlock`/`Terminator` shape redesign — D3's
// no-redesign call): LLVM 18 LangRef requires a `catchswitch` to be the
// FIRST non-phi instruction in its block AND the block to contain no
// other instructions. Since `CatchSwitch` IS the block's `terminator`,
// that rule reduces to: a catchswitch-terminated block's `body` must be
// EMPTY. Making this type-unrepresentable would require a per-terminator
// `BasicBlock` shape (the core-type redesign D3 explicitly declines);
// so — exactly as node.dag's D3 case — it is the checked structural
// invariant consumers (parse / normalize) evaluate fail-closed
// (INVARIANTS P3), never a silently-representable illegal state by
// convention. `BasicBlock` is NOT self-recursive (a block contains no
// blocks), so the faithful analog of node.dag's whole-tree
// `node_well_formed` is a single pure total LOCAL predicate (the
// `node_locally_well_formed` shape) — not artificial recursion.
fn terminator_is_catchswitch(t: Terminator) -> Bool {
  match t {
    CatchSwitch { result: _, parent_pad: _, handlers: _, unwind: _ } => true
    _ => false
  }
}

fn block_well_formed(b: BasicBlock) -> Bool {
  if terminator_is_catchswitch(t: b.terminator) {
    count(b.body) == 0
  } else {
    true
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// FidelityDisposition — the C5-fidelity model (DECISIONS C5-fidelity;
// T-4 authoring contract: "declare every surface feature's disposition").
// LLVM IR has negligible cosmetic surface, so F is essentially all-
// structural. The three dispositions are the exhaustive C5 partition;
// this mirrors std/diagnostic.dag `NoCorrectionReason`'s shape (a closed
// epistemic-partition sum). No Diagnostic / Result is referenced — the
// disposition is DATA the generic walker reads to know F; producing the
// fail-closed Diagnostic off-F is the walker's obligation, not this
// declarative model's (modeling notes: the open Result-carrier STOP does
// not touch a declarative LanguageModel).
//
//   - Modeled — ∈ F: Node-bearing, round-trips both directions.
//     Everything structural above (types, values, constants,
//     instructions, terminators, blocks, functions, globals, module
//     skeleton, atomic orderings, linkage).
//   - DeclaredNormalized — deliberately NOT in F; `emit∘ingest`
//     canonicalizes it, a DECLARED reviewable loss, never silent:
//       * SSA value spellings (`%1` vs `%x`) — the `result: Symbol`
//         def-site name and its `LocalRef` uses are K-1-opaque, so the
//         spelling is α-equivalent (the SAME rename-invariance
//         node.dag's content-hash fold already has — identity is the
//         structural one-def-site-per-name fact, the spelling
//         normalizes).
//       * non-entry basic-block textual order — the CFG defines order.
//       * comments (`;`), blank lines, horizontal whitespace.
//       * `data_layout` / `target_triple` opaque token (preserved
//         verbatim, not re-spelled — modeling notes / LlvmModule).
//       * purely-advisory metadata and non-semantic attributes
//         (`!dbg` debug info, optimization hints) — dropped by declared
//         choice; an ingest that needs them is a re-scope, not a guess.
//   - FailClosed — encountered but neither modeled nor declared-
//     normalizable ⇒ the walker emits a Diagnostic, NEVER a fabricated
//     parse (no-engine; the down-stack analog of the disassembly
//     fail-closed boundary):
//       * target-specific inline `asm` with constraint strings whose
//         semantics are not modeled.
//       * a SEMANTIC attribute that changes ABI/codegen meaning and is
//         not modeled (e.g. an unmodeled `byval`/`sret`/`align` whose
//         omission would silently change behavior) — fail-closed, never
//         dropped-as-if-advisory.
//       * IR that fails structural well-formedness (a phi after a non-
//         phi; >1 terminator; a use with no dominating def).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / INVARIANTS P1 / modeling-
// discipline.md §4) — classification: 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger —
// five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: the disposition is consumed uniformly by
//      the generic ingest/emit walker to know F; it is the shared C5
//      classifier, not a per-consumer scattered fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the only "data" reduction is a free
//      string describing the loss — the opaque-string no-engine smell;
//      the closed three-way partition is the faithful form. (The
//      `feature: FidelityFeature` payloads are a CLOSED enum, NOT an
//      opaque Symbol or readable string — so the per-feature disposition
//      is machine-consumable substrate DATA via `feature_disposition`,
//      not prose; P2/P3 — the generic walker reads the projection.)
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: this is the C5 epistemic-boundary
//      partition, not an algebra carrier; no richer std/ source — it IS
//      the lossless-core boundary definition.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a surface feature has EXACTLY ONE
//      disposition; modeled / declared-lost / fail-closed are mutually
//      exclusive — alternatives, never simultaneous coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: `Modeled` is nullary while the
//      other two carry a feature name-reference; heterogeneous, NOT one
//      `F<X>` over a declared set (not a Practice-7 projection).
// Terminal: the exhaustive, irreducible C5-fidelity partition — every
// surface feature answers structurally which side of F it is on.
type FidelityDisposition
  = Modeled
  | DeclaredNormalized { feature: FidelityFeature }
  | FailClosed { feature: FidelityFeature }


// FidelityFeature — the CLOSED set of C5-relevant LLVM-IR surface
// feature classes the model declares a disposition for (the prose
// bullets above, promoted to substrate so the generic ingest/emit
// walker reads DATA, not comments — P2/P3, a machine-consumable
// boundary, no opaque-Symbol/prose-only smell).
// Closed, and EXHAUSTIVE over the FailClosed boundaries the rest of the
// file routes here: the "valid-but-unmodeled LangRef X" surfaces that
// `LlvmType` / `LlvmInstruction` / `ConstExprOpcode` comments declare as
// the explicit FailClosed boundary are FIRST-CLASS variants here —
// `UnmodeledType` / `UnmodeledInstruction` / `UnmodeledConstExpr` —
// alongside `UnmodeledSemanticAttribute` / `UnmodeledInlineAsm` /
// `MalformedStructure`. So every "outside the modeled set ⇒ FailClosed"
// cross-reference resolves to a concrete `FidelityFeature` the
// `feature_disposition` projection maps — the generic walker reads a
// machine-consumable disposition for those boundaries (P2/P3), not a
// dangling prose route (a closed set is not enough unless it actually
// ENUMERATES the boundary classes the model defers to it). Not Symbol-keyed: a disposition TABLE/projection is
// authored data, and K-1 forbids minting a `Symbol` VALUE in a data
// literal — so a Symbol-keyed table is un-authorable (distinct from a
// declaration-class `result: Symbol` FIELD, which is a name-reference,
// not a mint — that is the SSA def-site idiom and is fine). A closed
// enum keyed by the feature CLASS is the faithful, authorable carrier.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: read uniformly by the generic walker via
//      `feature_disposition` to know F; a shared C5 classifier.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the reduction is a free string naming
//      the feature — the opaque-string no-engine smell; the closed
//      enumerated feature-class set is the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A: a C5 boundary taxonomy, not an algebra
//      carrier; no richer std/ source.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: the walker is handling EXACTLY ONE feature
//      class at a decision point; alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the classes are heterogeneous
//      (spelling / order / trivia / token / metadata / asm / attribute /
//      unmodeled-type / unmodeled-instruction / unmodeled-constexpr /
//      malformed / structural-core), NOT one `F<X>` over a declared set.
// Terminal: the closed C5 surface-feature-class set, EXHAUSTIVE over
// every FailClosed boundary the model routes here; its disposition is
// the total `feature_disposition` projection (single authority, M7).
type FidelityFeature
  = StructuralCore
  | SsaValueSpelling
  | BlockTextualOrder
  | LexicalTrivia
  | TargetConfigToken
  | AdvisoryMetadata
  | UnmodeledInlineAsm
  | UnmodeledSemanticAttribute
  | UnmodeledType
  | UnmodeledInstruction
  | UnmodeledConstExpr
  | MalformedStructure


// feature_disposition — the machine-consumable C5 boundary the generic
// ingest/emit walker reads (substrate DATA, not prose; INVARIANTS P2
// single-authority / P3 fail-closed). A total
// projection over the closed `FidelityFeature` set — adding a feature
// class is cost-of-change 1 here (Practice 7 / M7), never a scattered
// per-consumer comment.
fn feature_disposition(f: FidelityFeature) -> FidelityDisposition {
  match f {
    StructuralCore => Modeled
    SsaValueSpelling => DeclaredNormalized { feature: SsaValueSpelling }
    BlockTextualOrder => DeclaredNormalized { feature: BlockTextualOrder }
    LexicalTrivia => DeclaredNormalized { feature: LexicalTrivia }
    TargetConfigToken => DeclaredNormalized { feature: TargetConfigToken }
    AdvisoryMetadata => DeclaredNormalized { feature: AdvisoryMetadata }
    UnmodeledInlineAsm => FailClosed { feature: UnmodeledInlineAsm }
    UnmodeledSemanticAttribute => FailClosed { feature: UnmodeledSemanticAttribute }
    UnmodeledType => FailClosed { feature: UnmodeledType }
    UnmodeledInstruction => FailClosed { feature: UnmodeledInstruction }
    UnmodeledConstExpr => FailClosed { feature: UnmodeledConstExpr }
    MalformedStructure => FailClosed { feature: MalformedStructure }
  }
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Inhabitance — OWNED ELSEWHERE (P2 single-authority); DEFERRED, not
// declared here. (The ptx.dag / spice.dag deferral shape — a probe
// CONSUMES the canonical shape, it does not AUTHOR it.)
//
// This file declares ONLY the LLVM type/value/instruction VOCABULARY
// (the declaration-class grammar above). It does NOT declare, assert, or
// even restate which algebra an LLVM scalar grounds in. WHY-here vs
// where-owned:
//   - WHICH algebra a scalar inhabits is owned by the std/ scalar wave
//     (std/integer.dag, std/float.dag, std/logic.dag — T-3), by P2
//     single-authority: a concrete type declares its OWN inhabitance in
//     its OWN concept file (std/algebra.dag header SCOPE; node.dag T-1
//     disposition). An extdeps language file restating it — even as
//     prose — is a SECOND authority that forecloses that owner (the
//     exact P2 violation).
//   - The instance-value FORM (per-target `<Algebra><Concrete>` vs
//     abstract; the fn-body convention for spec-primitive ops) is the
//     OPEN, OPERATOR-PENDING **D2 STOP**: TASKS.md T-4 "i32 →
//     OrderedRing" DIRECTLY CONTRADICTS INVARIANTS P1 (~:42 — "operator
//     dispatch consumes abstract algebra facts, NOT a parallel
//     `OrderedRing<Word*>` substrate per width class"; R3 gate #19);
//     D2b's fn-body form (name-ref Atom / relaxed / new C1 carrier) is
//     one of three resolutions a worker may NOT pick.
//
// Per-file `data llvm_*: <Algebra><LlvmValue> = …` inhabitance
// instances are deliberately ABSENT: they ARE the D2-gated surface
// (P2 + INVARIANTS P1:42), and an operator-pending substrate-design
// decision is a HOLD/defer, never a 🟡-track-and-ship (🟡 is "richer
// source exists, substrate not ready", NOT "ship past an active
// escalation"). The no-6th-behavior probe result stands on the
// declarative model ALONE and does not need them.
//
// When D2 lands AND the std/ scalar owner declares the grounding, the
// connection (per-target SPELLING of the abstract algebra, not a
// re-inhabitance) is added at the ratified owner per the ratified form —
// CONSUMED here, never authored here. Empirical datum handed to the D2b
// decision (NOT a green-light): the deep nested-constructor + lambda-
// field `data`-instance form parses 0-diag under the frozen v2 seed
// (verified while probing) — so D2b is a FORM-authority decision, not a
// v2-admissibility blocker. Faithful grounding OBSERVATIONS for that
// owner (LLVM `iN` wraps ⇒ a finite ring, NOT order-compatible — the
// Float≠Field analog; `fcmp` NaN-unordered; pointers have no total
// numeric algebra) are carried to D2 / the std/ owner, NOT asserted as
// this file's authority.
//
// Scaffold "Owns: Inhabitance" is satisfied by this principled
// deferral-to-owner — NOT by improvising the D2-contested instance
// ahead of ratification (that improvisation IS the v3 cross-layer-drift
// failure mode v4 exists to kill — the STOP is the contribution).

// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Cost realization — per-instruction realization cost on an LLVM target
// (scaffold "Owns"). SINGLE AUTHORITY (M7 / Practice 7): a fn projecting
// the instruction structure to a kernel-ambient `Int` cost weight. It is
// NOT a parallel per-opcode table and NOT re-listed anywhere — the U1
// cost lens (lens/cost.dag, T-12; the homomorphism-with-cost spine
// THESIS:185-186) COMPOSES these per-instruction weights into a
// SymbolicCost; this file owns only the leaf realization datum the lens
// reads (the rust.dag-scaffold "cost realization" discipline). Weights
// are target-microarchitecture-relative latencies grounded in the LLVM
// `TargetTransformInfo` cost convention (the LangRef-adjacent cost
// authority): a memory/atomic op dominates an arithmetic op dominates a
// bitcast (a no-op move). The numbers are the conventional relative
// scale, not a fabricated precise cycle count (honest: the lens carries
// the symbolic composition; this is the per-leaf ground weight).
fn llvm_instruction_cost(i: LlvmInstruction) -> Int {
  match i {
    Binary { result: _, result_type: _, op: _, lhs: _, rhs: _ } => 1
    Conversion { result: _, result_type: _, op: OpBitCast, value: _ } => 0
    Conversion { result: _, result_type: _, op: _, value: _ } => 1
    IntCompare { result: _, predicate: _, lhs: _, rhs: _ } => 1
    FloatCompare { result: _, predicate: _, lhs: _, rhs: _ } => 1
    Alloca { result: _, allocated_type: _, element_count: _ } => 1
    Load { result: _, result_type: _, pointer: _, ordering: _ } => 4
    Store { value: _, pointer: _, ordering: _ } => 4
    GetElementPtr { result: _, source_type: _, base: _, indices: _ } => 1
    AtomicRmw { result: _, op: _, pointer: _, operand: _, ordering: _ } => 8
    CmpXchg { result: _, pointer: _, compare_value: _, new_value: _, success_ordering: _, failure_ordering: _ } => 8
    Fence { ordering: _ } => 4
    ExtractValue { result: _, aggregate: _, indices: _ } => 0
    InsertValue { result: _, aggregate: _, element: _, indices: _ } => 1
    ExtractElement { result: _, vector: _, index: _ } => 1
    InsertElement { result: _, vector: _, element: _, index: _ } => 1
    ShuffleVector { result: _, first: _, second: _, mask: _ } => 1
    Select { result: _, condition: _, if_true: _, if_false: _ } => 1
    Call { callee: _, arguments: _, result: _ } => 5
    Freeze { result: _, value: _ } => 0
    FNeg { result: _, result_type: _, operand: _ } => 1
    VaArg { result: _, result_type: _, va_list: _, arg_type: _ } => 2
    LandingPad { result: _, result_type: _, is_cleanup: _, clauses: _ } => 1
    CatchPad { result: _, catch_switch: _, args: _ } => 1
    CleanupPad { result: _, parent_pad: _, args: _ } => 1
  }
}
